    slug: 'philippines-content-assistant-morning-brief',
    title: 'A morning brief for a Philippines content assistant',
    excerpt: 'Turn the daily article target into a bounded assignment with sources, ownership, review timing, and a safe hold state.',
    opening: 'Daily publishing often goes wrong before anybody writes a sentence. A topic arrives without a reader question, an editor assumes the researcher knows which sources count, and the review window is mentioned only after the draft appears. A morning brief gives a Philippines-based content assistant a reliable starting point. It should make one article movable without pretending that every decision has already been made.',
    situation: 'The useful unit is one proposed route for one reader problem. Record the working title, intended slug, content family, reader, publication date, factual boundaries, permitted sources, and the person who will approve public claims. Add the expected handoff time in one time zone. If any required field is missing, the article remains in intake. A crowded calendar is not a reason to convert an incomplete idea into a writing assignment.',
    ownerDecision: 'The commissioning editor decides why the article belongs on the site, which service or operating problem it supports, and what claim the evidence must be able to sustain. The editor also owns publication priority and any exception involving a sensitive assertion. Those choices should appear in the brief rather than being inferred from a previous article or a private conversation.',
    assistantWork: 'The assistant can check the proposed slug against the corpus, assemble approved source material, draft a question-led outline, note missing evidence, and prepare a review copy. The role does not include inventing company experience, choosing a commercial promise, changing the campaign date, or releasing a held route. Clear limits make independent preparation possible because the assistant knows what can continue while a decision waits.',
    evidence: 'Attach source notes beside the parts of the outline they support. Each note should identify the publisher, page or document, relevant date, exact point observed, and any limitation. Company-supplied statements need an owner and approval state. A link alone is not evidence of fit. The morning brief should say whether a source establishes a fact, provides background, or merely suggests a question worth investigating.',
    failure: 'A weak brief usually produces one of two bad outcomes: a polished article that answers the wrong question, or a stalled draft whose writer keeps requesting context piecemeal. Watch for generic verbs such as research, improve, or optimize with no visible finish condition. Replace them with an observable artifact, such as a source table, a six-part outline, or a draft ready for a named editor.',
    example: 'Suppose the topic concerns handing customer support reports to a manager. The brief can define the reader as an operations lead, require examples about queue evidence and escalation, exclude invented performance results, and route refund or policy wording to the account owner. The assistant can then research and draft the operating steps while the manager retains customer-policy decisions. The division is specific enough to work across a time-zone handoff.',
    measure: 'Review brief quality through return reasons, not activity volume. Track missing reader definitions, route collisions, unavailable sources, unclear approval owners, and questions discovered after drafting began. Sample the original brief beside the returned draft. A shorter drafting time is useful only when the article also arrives within scope and with a traceable evidence record.',
    handoff: 'At the end of the work window, send the current route, draft link, sources checked, open questions, next permitted action, reviewer, and requested response time. Name the exact condition for resuming a held item. This lets a manager answer one decision without reconstructing the whole assignment and lets the next contributor distinguish completed preparation from publication approval.',
    close: 'A morning brief earns its place when it reduces ambiguity without hiding it. Keep the form short enough to use every day, but require the fields that protect public meaning. After several releases, remove questions that never influence work and strengthen the ones repeatedly associated with corrections. The routine should become more precise as the team learns, not longer by default.',
    headings: ['Define the article before the task', 'Keep decisions with the editor', 'Make evidence usable', 'Test the brief on a real handoff', 'Improve the intake from returns'],
