    slug: 'philippines-outsourced-subject-matter-question-routing',
    title: 'Subject-matter question routing for outsourced Philippines content work',
    excerpt: 'Turn uncertain claims into narrow, answerable questions for the owner who has authority to resolve them.',
    opening: 'Writers often lose more time waiting on broad requests for review than they spend resolving the underlying issue. A subject-matter expert receives an entire draft with no indication of what needs judgment. Question routing changes that handoff. A Philippines-based content specialist prepares the evidence and asks one bounded question of the person who can decide it.',
    situation: 'Open a question when a source conflict, company fact, regulated interpretation, service boundary, customer promise, or technical statement cannot be settled within the writer’s authority. Identify the exact route and sentence, what the draft currently says, what the available evidence supports, and the consequence of choosing each option. Keep unrelated copy out of the request.',
    ownerDecision: 'The editorial owner assigns subject domains and named backups. The subject expert decides the substantive point; the publisher decides how that answer appears publicly. Those may be different people. A response from somebody knowledgeable but unauthorized should remain advice until the accountable owner accepts it. The routing map needs this distinction.',
    assistantWork: 'A Philippines-based specialist can research the issue, compare approved sources, quote or paraphrase the relevant passages, propose cautious wording, and maintain the question queue. They may continue sections that do not depend on the answer. They should not infer approval from delay, combine separate expert opinions into a fabricated consensus, or choose the answer that best preserves the deadline.',
    evidence: 'Use a short packet with the proposed claim, source list, contradictions, known company context, open assumption, and requested decision. Mark confidential material so it does not flow into the public draft. If the question depends on current external guidance, include the observation date. The expert should be able to answer without searching for the affected paragraph.',
    failure: 'Sending please review creates avoidable delay because the recipient must diagnose the question before answering it. Tagging several owners without naming a decider creates a second failure: parallel comments arrive, but nobody knows which one controls. A third problem appears when the writer quietly changes the question after receiving an answer. Preserve the request and link any follow-up.',
    example: 'Suppose an article describes which customer complaint decisions a remote support hire can prepare. The writer can document factual workflow options, but the company’s escalation promise belongs to the service owner. The routed question identifies the proposed boundary and asks who approves exceptions. Once answered, the editor incorporates the decision without attributing broader results to the workflow.',
    measure: 'Track questions returned for missing context, time to first usable answer, reopened decisions, repeated categories, and articles held by unavailable owners. Avoid turning expert response time into an individual performance score without workload and priority context. The pattern should improve routing rules and briefing quality, not punish the people protecting sensitive claims.',
    handoff: 'Record the decision verbatim enough to preserve meaning, the decider, date, affected routes, and any condition or review date. Tell the writer exactly what may resume. If the answer changes company policy or multiple pages, open a separate governed change instead of quietly extending one article comment across the corpus.',
    close: 'The best routing system makes fewer questions necessary over time. Repeated answers should become approved examples or documented boundaries, with an owner and review date. Keep true exceptions visible. A knowledge base is useful only when it does not turn an old answer into permanent authority after the underlying policy changes.',
    headings: ['Recognize a real expert question', 'Send the smallest useful packet', 'Name one accountable decider', 'Preserve the answer and its scope', 'Convert repetition into guidance'],
